 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Precision**: Floating-point errors in coordinate calculation can cause `floor` and `ceil` to be equal for integer coordinates, resulting in duplicate corners. This is generally handled correctly by the algorithm but may waste computation [S1, unofficial].
 - **Out-of-Bounds**: If boundary handling is not implemented, accessing indices outside $[0, D-1]$ causes memory errors or undefined behavior [S4, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Input**: Points exactly on grid boundaries may have ambiguous corner selection if not handled consistently (e.g., floor vs ceil) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Integer Coordinates**: Points exactly on integer grid coordinates yield identical floor and ceil values, producing duplicate corner indices that the algorithm processes deterministically [S1, unofficial].
 
